  "skillId": "evaluate-trig-ratio",
  "atomType": "R",
  "theory": {
    "intro": "A calculator stores the value of every trigonometric ratio. Once the angle is known, a few keystrokes turn $\\sin\\theta$, $\\cos\\theta$ or $\\tan\\theta$ into a decimal, which is then rounded to the accuracy the question asks for.",
    "facts": [
      "The calculator must be in **degree** mode. In radian mode $\\sin 20^{\\circ}$ returns $0.9129$ instead of $0.3420$.",
      "For an acute angle, $\\sin\\theta$ and $\\cos\\theta$ always lie between $0$ and $1$, so a sine or cosine larger than $1$ signals a keying error.",
      "$\\tan\\theta$ has no upper limit: $\\tan 64^{\\circ}\\approx 2.0503$ and $\\tan 82.3^{\\circ}\\approx 7.3962$.",
      "In $12\\sin 13^{\\circ}$ the $12$ multiplies the ratio, so evaluate $\\sin 13^{\\circ}$ and then multiply.",
      "In $\\dfrac{9}{\\sin 57^{\\circ}}$ the ratio is the divisor, so evaluate $\\sin 57^{\\circ}$ and then divide $9$ by it.",
      "Round once, at the very end. Rounding the ratio first shifts the last digits of the answer."
    ],
    "steps": [
      "Check the calculator is in degree mode",
      "Evaluate the trigonometric ratio",
      "Round to the number of decimal places asked for"
    ]
  },
